    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Lidar data from an exposed fault in the Flowers Quarry, southern OR, were recorded with spacing of 10 mm over an area of 450m2. The prominent slip surface included in these data is analyzed using differential geometry, which admits point-by-point quantification of fundamental shapes to second order. The method maintains the spatial coherence of the data, enabling the formulation of boundary conditions for models, and yielding new insights into the mechanics of faulting. Repetitive patterns of the fundamental shapes occur with significant variations of principal normal curvature magnitudes and directions. At the centimeter scale preferential shapes do not emerge. At the decimeter scale a weak anisotropy that is symmetric with the inferred sliding direction is documented and slip-parallel streaks of either antiformal and domal structures or synformal and basinal structures are apparent. At the meter scale approximately developable surfaces with at least one near-zero principal normal curvature dominate the geometry of the slip surface. Angular variations in the normal vector across all scales yield spatial changes in resolved tractions and Coulomb stresses that are as great as 2MPa for idealized loading conditions. Solving the quasi-static boundary value problem of frictional sliding for heuristic models that represent the successive shapes documented on the slip surface we find that slip distributions are altered and slip magnitudes are less compared to developable surfaces. Principal stresses near the non-planar slip surfaces also are significantly altered by the existence of non-developable shapes in the direction of sliding.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
